kernel f(R) retained, reads 9D²−C³−RW ∈ K[R]; reducing mod V leaves
9D²≡C³+f(0). Evaluating at O, where C(O)=0 and every odd D vanishes
(D(O)=D(σO)=−D(O)), kills f(0). Hence, whenever the 3j residue has that
shape with D a polynomial, C≢0 mod V forces C ≡ (3D/C)² in K(E), i.e.
C|_E is the square of an odd function, i.e. C/p² is a square in K(u).
Concretely: ord_Q(C/p²) is even at every non-fixed point Q of E and
divisible by 4 at O and the three infinity points. The 14y collision
satisfies it by construction (C=r²−RL, r=pL², ord_O(C/p²)=3+1+0=4). The
admissible C family (even, deg≤8, weight≤0, edge i≤2j, C(0)=C_p8=0) has
exactly 13 coefficients, while the intersection cycle C·E has degree ≤24,
so 2-divisibility is expected to be a proper closed condition on that
family, with the "square minus RL" shape as the generic survivor. This is
a Kummer/2-descent condition, not a unit-parity condition: it does not
need a quadratic residue, it consumes the cubic one. It is NOT a theorem
