Universal proof. In Q[R,R⁻¹,r,L][[s]] write A=R³(1+ε), ε=s²C/R²+s³D/R³, and Φ=R⁵(1+ε)^{5/3} (binomial series), so Φ³=A⁵. The coefficients 5/3, 5/9, −5/81 give Φ=R⁵+(5/3)s²R³C+(5/3)s³R²D+(5/9)s⁴RC²+(10/9)s⁵CD+(5/81)s⁶(9D²−C³)/R+O(s⁷), and (1) turns the s⁶ term into (5/81)W, so B≡Φ mod s⁷. Differentiating Φ³=A⁵ gives Φ_X=(5A⁴/3Φ²)A_X with an X-independent factor, hence every 2×2 minor of (A,Φ) vanishes and every minor of (A,B) lies in s⁷; the polynomial ring injects into the localisation, so this holds in Q[R,r,L][s]. Substituting any R,r,L∈K[g,p] gives [A,B]≡0 mod s⁷. Equivalent route: B³≡A⁵ mod s⁷, 3B²[A,B]=[A,B³], and R¹⁰ is a nonzerodivisor, so the s-coefficients of [A,B] die inductively. Checked exactly: all three minors vanish through s⁶ in Q[R,r,L][s], and M_{Rr}, M_{RL} have nonzero s⁷ terms (a six-jet, not an arc); B³−A⁵≡(5/27)s⁶R⁹(RW+C³−9D²) mod s⁷ with R,C,D,W independent; on a random degree-1 instance the actual bracket equals the minor glue, vanishes through s⁶ and is nonzero at s⁷. Changing 5/81 or 5/9, or dropping D, fails.

